    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2000">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis — Magistro et al. 2026 (Capstone)&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Magistro, Borwein, Alvarez, Bonikowski, Loewen (2026). &quot;Attitudes toward artificial intelligence (AI) and globalization: Common microfoundations and political implications.&quot; American Journal of Political Science 70(1): 348–365. DOI: 10.1111/ajps.12959&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One-line claim: People evaluate AI and offshoring economic shocks through the same microfoundational lens — price sensitivity dominates employment sensitivity — but AI is viewed more favorably than offshoring, especially by Democrats.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Abstract (p. 348):** &quot;Advances in artificial intelligence (AI) are reshaping labor markets... Will AI, like globalization, challenge existing paradigms and trigger a backlash?&quot; — The AI-as-globalization-parallel thesis in its most compressed form.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Introduction (p. 350):** &quot;People are as responsive or more responsive to price changes than they are to employment changes, similarly for offshoring and AI; furthermore, they are always more sensitive to job losses than job gains.&quot; — Core result: price sensitivity &amp;gt; employment sensitivity; loss aversion confirmed symmetrically for both frames.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Introduction (p. 350):** &quot;A shift from worst-case to best-case scenarios can increase support for AI and offshoring by 36% and 34%, respectively... it can move people from weak opposition to weak support.&quot; — The politically actionable finding: crossing the neutral threshold (3/5), not just shifting means.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Introduction (p. 350–351):** Consumer identity mechanism — &quot;some individuals may prioritize their roles as consumers over their roles as workers when assessing economic changes.&quot; — The theoretical contribution distinguishing this from pure trade-attitudes literature.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Results (p. 358):** &quot;None of the effects differ between AI and offshoring, suggesting that people respond similarly to changes in prices and employment that result from either shock.&quot; — Counter-intuitive null: the frame shifts levels (AI favored) but not response slopes. Critical for Extension A.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Results (p. 358):** Authors report the weights vs. unweighted robustness check and endorse unweighted (minimal variation, higher power). → Extension B replication baseline.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Results (p. 358, fn. 13):** Null result on demographic subgroups (age, gender, income, education): &quot;no significant differences in the effects (AMCEs) of price and job changes across most subgroups.&quot; → Baseline for Extension A — standard cuts don't move slopes, raising the question of whether frame × job-type does.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Results (p. 359):** &quot;Republicans never cross the neutral line (3), they do move from opposed to neutral positions... Republicans are slightly more responsive than Democrats to price changes.&quot; → Nuances the partisan story; relevant for Extension C's prior trade attitudes moderator.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;| Citation | Why it matters |&lt;br/&gt;|---|---|&lt;br/&gt;| **Hainmueller, Hopkins &amp;amp; Yamamoto (2014)** *Political Analysis* 22(1): 1–30 | Foundational conjoint AMCE paper — required for understanding the estimation framework underlying the replication and all three extensions |&lt;br/&gt;| **Leeper, Hobolt &amp;amp; Tilley (2020)** *Political Analysis* 28(2): 207–21 | Marginal means for subgroup comparisons — the direct method reference for Figure 1 replication and Extensions A/C |&lt;br/&gt;| **Bansak et al. (2021a, 2021b)** | 2021a on satisficing; 2021b conjoint chapter — relevant to Extension B's attention-filter robustness check |&lt;br/&gt;| **Gallego &amp;amp; Kurer (2022)** *ARPS* 25: 463–84 | Synthesis on automation/AI in political behavior; voters conflate AI and trade; anchor for Extension A |&lt;br/&gt;| **Mutz (2021)** *POQ* 85(1): 101–22 | Trade rhetoric and misattribution of job loss; framing mechanism for Extension A |&lt;br/&gt;| **Naoi &amp;amp; Kume (2015)** *CPS* 48(10): 1293–1317 | Worker-vs.-consumer identity; price benefits activate consumer identity — core motivation for Extension C's prior-trade-attitudes split |&lt;br/&gt;| **Baker (2003, 2005)** *World Politics* / *AJPS* | Consumption-based theory of trade preferences; baseline theory for Extension C |&lt;br/&gt;| **Di Tella &amp;amp; Rodrik (2020)** *Economic Journal* 130(628): 1008–30 | Labor shock exposure and demand for trade protection via survey; misattribution mechanism for Extension A |&lt;br/&gt;| **Walter (2021)** | Politicization of globalization and activation of latent grievances — theoretical rationale for Extension C's median split on prior trade attitudes |&lt;br/&gt;| **Magistro et al. (2024)** *PSRM* 12(4): 783–98 | Authors' prior paper on automation (not generative AI); established price sensitivity finding; this paper is the direct extension to the AI/offshoring comparison |&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data and methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Design novelty (p. 355):** Bi-national simultaneous conjoint (US + Canada, N=6,056, YouGov) randomizing the *source* of the economic shock (AI vs. offshoring) within otherwise identical conjoint tables. No prior study directly compares these two frames with matched-country samples.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **MM vs. AMCE choice (p. 357–358):** The decision to report MMs as main results (rather than AMCEs) is non-obvious. MMs capture absolute support levels and are robust to reference-category choice; AMCEs capture relative effects of attribute changes. This distinction maps directly onto Extension B's OLS-vs.-ordered-logit question — OLS recovers marginal effects (AMCE-like), ordered logit recovers latent-scale effects (closer to MM logic).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters for extensions&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Extension A:** &quot;Once we compute slopes (the changes in support divided by the changes in jobs), respondents are most responsive to customer service jobs.&quot; (p. 358) — The paper notes job-type heterogeneity in responsiveness but never interacts job type with the AI/offshoring treatment frame. That gap is Extension A. Students should compute: (AI MM − Offshoring MM) separately for DS jobs vs. factory jobs, then test equality.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Extension A:** Authors flag &quot;higher-order interactions&quot; (frame × economic conditions) as future work they couldn't pursue due to power concerns (fn. 14, p. 359). Extension A is exactly this. N=6,056 × 4 tasks gives adequate power for a two-way frame × job-type interaction.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Extension B:** The SI robustness figures (E1–E4) already model the attention-filter check and country split. Extension B students should treat these as the template for their specification grid: (attention filter) × (weights) × (OLS vs. ologit) = 8 cells.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Extension C:** &quot;Parties tend to activate and make salient attitudes to which people are already disposed&quot; (Bonikowski 2017, cited p. 354). Prior trade attitudes (tables_policygr_5) are the latent disposition; the offshoring/AI frame is the activation mechanism. Median split on tables_policygr_5 → estimate MMs by treat_offshoring within each group → test whether the AI-preference gap is larger among pro-traders.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Relevance to the capstone — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. **Figure 1 is the replication target.** The cregg::cj() call with estimate=&quot;mm&quot; and by=~treat_offshoring exactly reproduces it. reference_code/main.R lines ~133–165 have the working code.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. **Extension A has a clean theoretical gap to fill.** The paper reports job-type differences in responsiveness (slope heterogeneity) but never interacts treatment frame with job type. This is a 10-line addition to the replication code.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. **Extension B is scaffolded in the paper's own SI.** The attention-filter and country robustness checks are already done (Figures E1–E4, B1). Students are extending a completed sensitivity analysis into a full specification curve — the paper gives them the first two cells for free.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. **Extension C has explicit theoretical grounding in the paper.** The &quot;latent attitudes activation&quot; framing (Walter 2021; Bonikowski 2017) cited on p. 354 is the exact mechanism. tables_policygr_5 (trade liberalization support) is in the dataset and has not been used as a moderator anywhere in the paper or appendix.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;5. **The null on framing slopes (Red-5) is the most important result to understand before running extensions.** If AI and offshoring frames produce identical *slopes* but different *intercepts*, then Extensions A and C should be framed as asking whether the intercept gap varies by job type or prior attitude — not whether the responsiveness pattern differs.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;6. **The price-sensitivity finding is robust and surprising.** Students should address it in their Discussion — their extensions will either confirm it (price sensitivity holds within subgroups/job types) or reveal heterogeneity the main paper missed.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Generated: 2026-04-29&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
