        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2018"/>
        <dc:title>Causal Inference in Conjoint Analysis: Understanding Multidimensional Choices via Stated Preference Experiments</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Survey experiments are a core tool for causal inference. Yet, the design of classical survey experiments prevents them from identifying which components of a multidimensional treatment are influential. Here, we show how
              conjoint analysis
              , an experimental design yet to be widely applied in political science, enables researchers to estimate the causal effects of multiple treatment components and assess several causal hypotheses simultaneously. In conjoint analysis, respondents score a set of alternatives, where each has randomly varied attributes. Here, we undertake a formal identification analysis to integrate conjoint analysis with the potential outcomes framework for causal inference. We propose a new causal estimand and show that it can be nonparametrically identified and easily estimated from conjoint data using a fully randomized design. The analysis enables us to propose diagnostic checks for the identification assumptions. We then demonstrate the value of these techniques through empirical applications to voter decision making and attitudes toward immigrants.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2014</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Causal Inference in Conjoint Analysis</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S1047198700013589/type/journal_article</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 14:45:00</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>https://www.cambridge.org/core/terms</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>1-30</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>hainmuellerCausalInferenceConjoint2014</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:1047-1987,%201476-4989">
        <prism:volume>22</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Political Analysis</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1093/pan/mpt024</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>1</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Polit. anal.</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 1047-1987, 1476-4989</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2156">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis: Hainmueller, Hopkins &amp;amp; Yamamoto (2014)&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hainmueller, Jens, Daniel J. Hopkins, and Teppei Yamamoto. 2014. &quot;Causal Inference in Conjoint Analysis: Understanding Multidimensional Choices via Stated Preference Experiments.&quot; Political Analysis 22(1): 1–30. https://doi.org/10.1093/pan/mpt024&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One-line claim: Conjoint analysis — in which respondents evaluate profiles with randomly varied attributes — enables nonparametric identification and estimation of the average marginal component effect (AMCE) of each attribute on preferences, allowing researchers to simultaneously test multiple causal hypotheses about multidimensional choices.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. (p. 2) Standard survey experiments identify only the catch-all effect of a composite treatment; they cannot decompose which components drive observed effects — the core problem conjoint solves.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. (p. 3) The AMCE is formally defined and shown to be nonparametrically identified under three assumptions (stability/no carryover, no profile-order effects, and randomization of profiles) that are either guaranteed by design or partially testable.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. (p. 28) In the conclusion: conjoint experiments separately identify component-specific causal effects by randomly manipulating multiple attributes simultaneously, estimated by simple OLS with clustered SEs — no special software required.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. (p. 20) In the immigration conjoint, education and English fluency dominate preferences: immigrants with a B.A. are 0.18 percentage points more likely to be preferred for admission than those with no formal education (SE = 0.02).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. (p. 20) Country-of-origin effects are typically small and statistically insignificant once other attributes are controlled — only Sudan, Somalia, and Iraq reduce admission probability relative to India. Challenges single-attribute designs that found large country-of-origin effects.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. (p. 22) The strongest single AMCE is the penalty for immigrants with no plans to look for work — larger than any country-of-origin effect. Suggests labor-market integration signals dominate cultural cues when evaluated simultaneously.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Scheve, K., and M. Slaughter. 2001. &quot;Labor Market Competition and Individual Preferences over Immigration Policy.&quot; Review of Economics and Statistics 83(1): 133–45. [Labor market competition / factor-proportions model of immigration attitudes — essential precursor for economic threat framing in globalization backlash research.]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Citrin, J., D. P. Green, C. Muste, and C. Wong. 1997. &quot;Public Opinion toward Immigration Reform: The Role of Economic Motivations.&quot; Journal of Politics 59(3): 858–81. [Economic vs. cultural threat models of immigration opinion; used here to frame the multi-hypothesis structure of the conjoint.]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Brader, T., N. Valentino, and E. Suhay. 2008. &quot;Is It Immigration or the Immigrants? The Emotional Influence of Groups on Public Opinion and Political Action.&quot; American Journal of Political Science 52(4): 959–78. [Canonical example of the aliasing problem: ethnicity, name, and country bundled together; used in this paper as the motivating design failure.]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Hainmueller, J., and M. J. Hiscox. 2010. &quot;Attitudes toward Highly Skilled and Low-Skilled Immigration: Evidence from a Survey Experiment.&quot; American Political Science Review 104(1): 61–84. [Direct precursor — skills-based immigration experiment; used here to show limits of single-attribute designs even when clean.]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data and methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The AMCE estimand itself (p. 11): a formally novel causal quantity — the marginal effect of one attribute averaged over the joint randomization distribution of all co-attributes — integrated into the potential outcomes framework. Nonparametrically identified from conjoint data without behavioral modeling assumptions. This is the paper's core methodological contribution.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. (p. 12) The AMCE is defined with respect to a chosen distribution of co-attributes; the authors suggest using the real-world distribution to improve external validity. For a job-loss conjoint, this means anchoring attribute frequencies to actual CZ-level automation exposure or trade-shock intensity distributions, rather than uniform random.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. (p. 13) Conditional AMCEs by respondent characteristics are nonparametrically identified. Directly adaptable: split by respondent CZ's automation-exposure quartile, trade-shock tercile, or union status to test whether economically vulnerable respondents weight job-loss cause (automation vs. trade vs. offshoring) differently.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. (p. 25) Too few attributes cause aliasing — respondents use the included attribute as a proxy for omitted correlated ones. For a job-loss conjoint, cause-of-job-loss (automation, trade, domestic competition) must be a standalone attribute, not left implicit, or all other attribute effects will be confounded.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. (p. 27) Conjoint reduces social desirability bias because respondents can find multiple justifications for any choice. Useful for globalization/trade backlash research where nativist or protectionist responses may be suppressed in direct-question formats.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- This paper is the methodological foundation for any conjoint experiment on job-loss attitudes. The AMCE framework is the right estimand when testing whether cause-of-job-loss (automation vs. trade) has independent effects on support for redistribution, trade restrictions, or political candidates.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The immigration experiment is a direct design template: attributes map onto job-loss scenario attributes (cause, worker characteristics, industry, region, retraining availability), and the AMCE approach allows simultaneous estimation of all attribute effects on stated policy preferences.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The finding that country-of-origin effects disappear once skills/employment plans are included is an object lesson for job-loss research: media framing effects may similarly wash out once respondents are given full economic context — worth testing with a conjoint that varies cause, industry sector, and wage level simultaneously.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Conditional AMCEs by ethnocentrism (Fig. 4) provide a direct model for splitting by CZ automation exposure: does the treatment effect of &quot;automation&quot; (vs. &quot;trade&quot;) on policy attitudes differ for respondents in high-exposure vs. low-exposure labor markets?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The diagnostic battery (carryover, profile-order, attribute-order, atypical profiles) is a checklist to build into any job-loss conjoint pre-analysis plan.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Social desirability reduction is particularly relevant for automation backlash research: respondents may hesitate to endorse protectionist or anti-automation policies directly but can do so indirectly through profile choices.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
