        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2003"/>
        <dc:title>Measuring Subgroup Preferences in Conjoint Experiments</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Conjoint analysis is a common tool for studying political preferences. The method disentangles patterns in respondents’ favorability toward complex, multidimensional objects, such as candidates or policies. Most conjoints rely upon a fully randomized design to generate average marginal component effects (AMCEs). They measure the degree to which a given value of a conjoint profile feature increases, or decreases, respondents’ support for the overall profile relative to a baseline, averaging across all respondents and other features. While the AMCE has a clear causal interpretation (about the
              effect
              of features), most published conjoint analyses also use AMCEs to describe
              levels
              of favorability. This often means comparing AMCEs among respondent subgroups. We show that using conditional AMCEs to describe the degree of subgroup agreement can be misleading as regression interactions are sensitive to the reference category used in the analysis. This leads to inferences about subgroup differences in preferences that have arbitrary sign, size, and significance. We demonstrate the problem using examples drawn from published articles and provide suggestions for improved reporting and interpretation using marginal means and an omnibus F-test. Given the accelerating use of these designs in political science, we offer advice for best practice in analysis and presentation of results.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>04/2020</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S1047198719000305/type/journal_article</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 14:45:14</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>https://www.cambridge.org/core/terms</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>207-221</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>leeperMeasuringSubgroupPreferences2020</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2087">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis — Leeper, Hobolt &amp;amp; Tilley (2020)&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Leeper, Thomas J., Sara B. Hobolt, and James Tilley. 2020. &quot;Measuring Subgroup Preferences in Conjoint Experiments.&quot; Political Analysis 28(2): 207–221. https://doi.org/10.1017/pan.2019.30&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One-line claim: Conditional AMCEs are invalid for comparing preferences across respondent subgroups because they are arbitrarily sensitive to reference category choice; marginal means and omnibus F-tests should be used instead.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. (p. 4) The core problem: &quot;We show that a difference in two (or more) subgroups' favorability toward a conjoint feature — like a difference in willingness to support a particular type of immigrant between high and low ethnocentrism respondents — is only rarely reflected in the difference-in-AMCEs.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. (p. 4) Headline danger: &quot;Reference category choices can make similar preferences look dissimilar and dissimilar preferences look similar.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. (p. 13) Formal mechanism: The difference-in-AMCEs equals the difference in preferences only when the reference-category means are identical across subgroups (β2 ≡ β0) — a condition the standard AMCE design never verifies and cannot guarantee.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. (p. 22) Core prescription: &quot;We suggest that conjoint analyses should report not only average marginal component effects (AMCEs) but also descriptive quantities about levels of favorability... Marginal means contain all of the information provided by AMCEs and more.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. (p. 15–16) Teele et al. replication: The difference-in-AMCEs for Democrat vs. Republican preferences over female candidates (0.089) doubles the true preference difference (0.045) because it adds the reference-category (male) difference on top of the female-candidate difference.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. (p. 16) Bechtel &amp; Scheve reanalysis: With the same data, different reference category choices can produce differences-in-AMCEs that are large positive, small positive, negligible, small negative, or large negative — a complete reversal of inference direction.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. (p. 21) Omnibus F-test catches published errors: Bechtel &amp; Scheve's claim that four countries &quot;largely agree&quot; is rejected by a nested model comparison (F(54, 67982)=3.72, p≤0.00), demonstrating the practical stakes of using the wrong method.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Hainmueller, Hopkins &amp; Yamamoto (2014)** — Political Analysis 22:1–30. The foundational conjoint paper introducing the AMCE and the randomized design. Essential baseline for any conjoint study.&lt;br/&gt;- **Bansak, Hainmueller &amp; Hangartner (2016)** — Science 354(6309):217–222. Asylum-seeker attitudes conjoint across European countries with subgroup analysis by left-right ideology and income — directly relevant to globalization-backlash work.&lt;br/&gt;- **Gallego &amp; Marx (2017)** — Journal of European Public Policy 24(7):1027–1047. Labor market reform preferences via conjoint — close analogue for automation/trade policy conjoint designs.&lt;br/&gt;- **Egami &amp; Imai (2018)** — JASA forthcoming. Formal treatment of causal interaction in factorial/conjoint experiments; the companion piece to read alongside Leeper et al. for subgroup analysis.&lt;br/&gt;- **Ballard-Rosa, Martin &amp; Scheve (2016)** — JOP 79(1):1–16. Tax policy conjoint with rich subgroup comparisons by demographics and political orientation — template for exposure-based subgroup splits.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;None warranted. The paper's contribution is methodological/statistical (showing a property of regression with interactions), not a novel data source or genuinely novel estimator. The cregg R package is an implementation of existing quantities (marginal means, F-tests), not a novel method.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. **Causal vs. descriptive distinction** (p. 5): Any conjoint on automation/trade attitudes will be used descriptively (&quot;workers in high-automation CZs prefer X policy&quot;). That descriptive use demands marginal means, not AMCEs, especially for comparisons across exposure groups.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. **Three-part toolkit** (p. 17): When comparing high-automation vs. low-automation workers in a conjoint, use (a) conditional marginal means per subgroup, (b) differences in MMs to estimate preference gaps, and (c) omnibus F-test for overall heterogeneity across all policy features.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. **cregg R package** (p. 23): Implements all of the above. Plan to use `cj()` with `by =` argument for subgroup MMs and `cj_anova()` for omnibus tests in the automation conjoint analysis.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. **Reference category diagnostic** (p. 4, 9–10): Before reporting any subgroup AMCE comparison, run the sensitivity check — try multiple reference categories and verify that the direction and significance of subgroup differences are stable. If unstable, switch to MMs.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;5. **Design implication**: If the conjoint includes features like &quot;policy covers workers displaced by automation&quot; vs. &quot;policy covers workers displaced by trade,&quot; and you split by actual CZ automation exposure, the subgroup comparison must use MMs. Conditional AMCEs will be confounded by how exposed vs. unexposed workers rate the reference policy feature.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Conjoint design validity check**: Any conjoint study comparing worker subgroups (e.g., high-automation CZ vs. low, manufacturing vs. services) MUST use marginal means for subgroup comparisons, not conditional AMCEs. This is a non-optional methodological requirement per Leeper et al.&lt;br/&gt;- **Subgroup split variables**: Standard splits in the literature (education, left-right ideology, income) are all susceptible to the reference category problem; the paper's examples directly parallel the types of subgroup analyses common in trade/automation attitudes research.&lt;br/&gt;- **Null results threat**: Published findings that automation-exposed vs. unexposed workers &quot;have similar preferences&quot; toward compensation policies may be artifacts of reference category choice — worth checking in existing conjoint studies on redistribution/trade.&lt;br/&gt;- **Pre-registration implication**: Pre-registering the use of marginal means (not AMCEs) as the primary estimand for subgroup comparisons is now a best-practice requirement that reviewers will look for in any conjoint paper.&lt;br/&gt;- **F-test for globalization backlash heterogeneity**: An omnibus F-test across all conjoint features is the right way to test whether automation-exposed workers differ *overall* from non-exposed workers in their policy preferences — more credible than cherry-picking feature-level comparisons.&lt;br/&gt;- **Gallego &amp; Marx as a template**: Their labor market reform conjoint with left-right subgroups is the closest published analogue; checking whether they report MMs will indicate whether the paper is methodologically compliant by Leeper et al. standards.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
