        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2005"/>
        <dc:title>Automation, Digitalization, and Artificial Intelligence in the Workplace: Implications for Political Behavior</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>New technologies have been a key driver of labor market change in recent decades. There are renewed concerns that technological developments in areas such as robotics and artificial intelligence will destroy jobs and create political upheaval. This article reviews the vibrant debate about the economic consequences of recent technological change and then discusses research about how digitalization may affect political participation, vote choice, and policy preferences. It is increasingly well established that routine workers have been the main losers of recent technological change and disproportionately support populist parties. However, at the same time, digitalization also creates a large group of economic winners who support the political status quo. The mechanisms connecting technology-related workplace risks to political behavior and policy demands are less well understood. Voters may fail to fully comprehend the relative importance of different causes of structural economic change and misattribute blame to other factors. We conclude with a list of pressing research questions.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2022-05-12</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Automation, Digitalization, and Artificial Intelligence in the Workplace</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-polisci-051120-104535</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 14:45:24</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>463-484</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>gallegoAutomationDigitalizationArtificial2022a</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:1094-2939,%201545-1577">
        <prism:volume>25</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Annual Review of Political Science</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1146/annurev-polisci-051120-104535</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>1</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Annu. Rev. Polit. Sci.</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 1094-2939, 1545-1577</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2154">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis: Gallego &amp;amp; Kurer (2022) — Automation, Digitalization, and AI in the Workplace&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;**Gallego, Aina, and Thomas Kurer. 2022. &quot;Automation, Digitalization, and Artificial Intelligence in the Workplace: Implications for Political Behavior.&quot; *Annual Review of Political Science* 25: 463–484.**&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;**One-line claim:** Routine workers who lose out to technological change turn toward the populist radical right, but the overall backlash is smaller than expected because a large, politically conservative group of technology winners counterbalances losers — and losers misattribute their decline to trade and immigration rather than technology, driven by psychological biases and strategic elite framing.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- &quot;The most consistent finding of the literature is that those who lose out to technological innovation turn against the political status quo in general and toward the populist radical right in particular.&quot; (p.464)&lt;br/&gt;- &quot;the presence of an important but somewhat neglected group of 'ordinary winners' leads us to conclude that technological change might result in less electoral backlash than commonly assumed. Yet, it may be an important force in political realignments.&quot; (p.464)&lt;br/&gt;- Workers may misattribute decline to technology due to: (a) complexity/low visibility of tech change, (b) psychological bias toward in-group/out-group framing, and (c) strategic mobilization by political entrepreneurs who downplay technology. (p.464)&lt;br/&gt;- &quot;While disentangling the effects of automation and globalization is difficult, most existing studies attribute the bulk of the decline in U.S. manufacturing employment to the former rather than the latter. Yet we do not see populists campaign against technology or automation.&quot; (Rodrik 2018, quoted p.475)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Routine workers are &quot;middle class&quot; voters with political resources — they have both the motive and means to carry dissatisfaction into the political arena. (p.471)&lt;br/&gt;- Authors' own panel study (UK + ICT investment data): ordinary winners of technological change are &quot;clear-cut supporters of the political status quo,&quot; especially when center-right is in power. (p.472)&lt;br/&gt;- At-risk workers do not demand active labor market policies or education spending — the policies economists recommend go unsupported by their intended beneficiaries (Busemeyer &amp; Sahm 2021; Kurer &amp; Häusermann 2022). (p.474)&lt;br/&gt;- Gingrich (2019): compensation policies (early retirement, in-kind spending) do not prevent the turn to populist parties among high-RTI workers — compensating workers is not effective at preventing protest voting. (p.471)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Acemoglu &amp; Restrepo (2020)** — shift-share robot exposure at the commuting zone level; foundational identification strategy for CZ-level automation research.&lt;br/&gt;- **Frey et al. (2018)** — robotization at local labor market level increased Trump support in 2016; key paper linking CZ automation to voting outcomes.&lt;br/&gt;- **Wu (2021a,b)** — automation risk workers are more likely to oppose trade and immigration but not to demand tech-related spending; direct survey evidence of misattribution.&lt;br/&gt;- **Rodrik &amp; di Tella (2020)** — experimental study: citizens exposed to stories of tech-displaced workers become more supportive of protectionism; highly relevant to conjoint design.&lt;br/&gt;- **Kurer &amp; Häusermann (2022)** — subjective automation risk measure (&quot;how likely is your job to be automated in the next 10 years&quot;); useful instrument for survey design.&lt;br/&gt;- **Kaihovaara &amp; Im (2020)** — European workers in high-RTI occupations more likely to support trade protectionism and immigration restrictions.&lt;br/&gt;- **Colantone &amp; Stanig (2019)** — trade exposure and economic nationalism/populism; key reference for globalization backlash literature.&lt;br/&gt;- **Anelli et al. (2021)** — counterfactual individual automation exposure measure (pre-automation occupation probabilities × regional robotization); increases radical right support across 13 W. European countries.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Anelli et al. (2021)** measure of individual automation vulnerability: combines pre-automation probability of working in a given occupation with Frey &amp; Osborne automation risk scores and regional robot adoption pace. This captures both direct displacement and indirect displacement (labor market entrants blocked from manufacturing), going beyond RTI or current-job exposure measures. (p.471)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Conjoint design for attributional asymmetry:** The three-way causal comparison (technology vs. trade vs. immigration) is directly testable. A conjoint could randomize the stated cause of a worker's job loss and measure downstream policy demands and blame attribution. This paper provides the theoretical grounding for why automation should generate weaker policy-specific demand than trade/immigration.&lt;br/&gt;- **Elite framing conjoint arm:** Parties cue misattribution by making certain explanations cognitively available. A conjoint or survey experiment could randomize the elite frame (politician blaming tech vs. blaming trade) and measure effects on respondent attribution and policy preference.&lt;br/&gt;- **Shock visibility as a treatment:** The visibility/gradualism distinction (sudden factory closure vs. gradual automation) could be operationalized as a vignette dimension in a conjoint — does sudden vs. gradual job loss description change policy demand or blame attribution?&lt;br/&gt;- **CZ-level automation research:** Clustering of automation costs in specific communities is the key mechanism linking aggregate technology change to local political reactions. This paper endorses shift-share designs at the CZ level (Acemoglu &amp; Restrepo 2020; Frey et al. 2018) — must build automation exposure variable at CZ level using IFR robot data.&lt;br/&gt;- **Winners as a moderating group:** The &quot;ordinary winners&quot; argument suggests that aggregate effects on vote shares will be smaller than individual-level studies imply. A CZ-level design should account for the winner/loser composition of the local labor market, not just the exposure to routine job loss.&lt;br/&gt;- **Subjective vs. objective automation risk:** Kurer &amp; Häusermann's subjective measure could supplement RTI in a survey conjoint; useful for checking whether workers who feel at risk react differently to job-loss vignettes by cause.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Conjoint instrument:** This paper provides the theoretical core of a vignette conjoint: randomize cause of job loss (automation vs. trade/offshoring vs. immigration) and measure policy demand, blame attribution, and voting intention. The misattribution hypothesis predicts that automation will generate weaker policy-specific demand (retraining, UBI) but stronger demand for protectionism/immigration restriction relative to trade/immigration vignettes.&lt;br/&gt;- **Mechanism heterogeneity:** The three misattribution channels (psychological, political-entrepreneurial, and visibility/shock) can each be operationalized as conjoint dimensions — testing which channel is most behaviorally consequential is a contribution.&lt;br/&gt;- **CZ-level design:** Confirms that shift-share robot exposure at CZ level (Acemoglu &amp; Restrepo 2020) is the workhorse identification strategy. Frey et al. (2018) and Anelli et al. (2021) are the key prior papers to engage.&lt;br/&gt;- **Winners must be modeled:** Any CZ-level design should incorporate local labor market composition (routine vs. nonroutine job growth) — not just automation losers — to avoid overstating the backlash effect.&lt;br/&gt;- **Why globalization backlash is partly an automation story:** The misattribution mechanism is the key link between this paper and globalization backlash literature. Rising anti-trade attitudes in deindustrialized communities may partly reflect automation anxiety being redirected toward trade policy — this paper gives the mechanism, Rodrik &amp; di Tella (2020) give the experimental evidence.&lt;br/&gt;- **Policy demand gap:** At-risk workers do not demand the policies designed to help them (Busemeyer &amp; Sahm 2021; Kurer &amp; Häusermann 2022). This is itself a testable implication in a conjoint — vary whether the respondent is told about a policy designed &quot;specifically for automation&quot; vs. generic redistribution.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>
           <z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>automation</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
