        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2013"/>
        <dc:title>Workers or Consumers? A Survey Experiment on the Duality of Citizens’ Interests in the Politics of Trade</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>What determines the attitude of citizens toward international trade in advanced industrialized nations? The question raises an intriguing paradox for low-income citizens in developed economies. Increasing imports pose the most severe threat to job security for low-income citizens, who, on the other hand, reap the greatest benefits from cheaper imports as consumers. This article considers the role of dual identities that citizens have as both income-earners and consumers, and investigates how attitudes toward trade differ depending on which aspect of respondents’ lives—that is, work versus consumption—is activated. The results of an originally designed survey experiment conducted in Japan during the recession suggest that the activation of a consumer perspective is associated with much higher support for free trade. In particular, those respondents who have lower levels of job security are the ones who, with consumer-priming, increase their support for foreign imports.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>09/2015</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Workers or Consumers?</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0010414015574879</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 14:46:20</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>1293-1317</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>naoiWorkersConsumersSurvey2015</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0010-4140,%201552-3829">
        <prism:volume>48</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Comparative Political Studies</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1177/0010414015574879</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>10</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>Comparative Political Studies</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0010-4140, 1552-3829</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2153">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis — Naoi &amp;amp; Kume (2015)&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Naoi, Megumi, and Ikuo Kume. 2015. &quot;Workers or Consumers? A Survey Experiment on the Duality of Citizens' Interests in the Politics of Trade.&quot; Comparative Political Studies 48(10): 1293–1317.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One-line claim: Citizens hold dual worker/consumer identities that produce conflicting trade preferences, and experimentally activating the consumer identity can substantially raise support for free trade — especially among low-income citizens facing the highest job-loss risk from imports.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;br/&gt;- Core mechanism (p.1294): Consumer identity activation mobilizes higher support for free trade precisely among low-income citizens who face the greatest threat of job loss — the &quot;torn between the duality of interests&quot; argument.&lt;br/&gt;- Headline finding (p.1303): Consumer priming raised pro-trade responses by 9.5pp and lowered opposition by 13pp, significant at 1%.&lt;br/&gt;- Reversed H-O prediction (p.1304): Consumer priming reverses Heckscher-Ohlin patterns — low-income respondents (default protectionists) showed the largest pro-trade increase (+12pp) under consumer priming.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting&lt;br/&gt;- Asymmetric priming (p.1303): Producer priming had only weak effects (-3.8pp, sig. at 5%); respondents were already saturated with protectionist discourse. Consumer framing is the novel counterfactual lever.&lt;br/&gt;- Shopping-habits heterogeneity (p.1305): &quot;Cheap Shoppers&quot; (no luxury brand ownership) increased pro-trade support by 12.5pp vs. only 6pp for &quot;Luxury Shoppers,&quot; validating the Engel's Law mechanism.&lt;br/&gt;- Partisan mediation (p.1307): DPJ supporters (exposed to &quot;quality of life&quot; campaign messaging) increased trade support by 12pp with consumer priming; LDP supporters showed no effect. Elite framing gates identity activation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;br/&gt;- Autor, Dorn &amp; Hanson (2013) — &quot;The China Syndrome&quot; — cited on pp.1294, 1299. Core reference for CZ-level import competition and employment decline.&lt;br/&gt;- Hiscox (2006) — Framing experiment (US); cited p.1294. Parallel design; found job-threat framing raises protectionism but consumer-benefit framing did not mobilize support (opposite to Japan). Contrast case.&lt;br/&gt;- Mansfield &amp; Mutz (2009) — Sociotropic trade attitudes; cited p.1296. The paper explicitly contrasts its self-interest mechanism against M&amp;M's out-group/sociotropic argument — key theoretical fork for conjoint design.&lt;br/&gt;- Margalit (2013) — Social policy preferences from the Great Recession; cited p.1300. Individual-level job loss → policy attitude shifts. Directly relevant to automation/backlash project.&lt;br/&gt;- Baker (2005, 2009) — Consumer tastes and trade policy beliefs; cited p.1295. Foundational for the consumption-side identification strategy.&lt;br/&gt;- Scheve &amp; Slaughter (2001) — Globalization and perceptions of American workers; cited p.1294. Standard H-O baseline for trade attitude models.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; methods&lt;br/&gt;- Visual image priming design (p.1300): Photos of workplaces vs. retail stores prime producer/consumer identity without explicit trade framing or valence. This is a genuinely novel manipulation that separates identity salience from argument supply — most prior work used text-based framing. N=1,200, blocked randomization on gender/age/income/prefecture, Yahoo! Japan panel (December 2008).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;br/&gt;- Dual-identity conjoint attribute (p.1294): A conjoint experiment on trade/automation could vary whether scenarios are described in worker terms (job loss, sector displacement) vs. consumer terms (price effects, import availability). The paper shows these frames produce 13–22pp swings in the same low-income respondents.&lt;br/&gt;- Heterogeneous effects by job security (p.1304/1306): In CZ-level automation research, residents of high-robot-exposure CZs may be simultaneously the most threatened workers AND the most price-sensitive consumers. This dual exposure could suppress or amplify observed backlash — worth testing as an interaction in observational data.&lt;br/&gt;- Consumer identity as backlash moderator (p.1306): &quot;Citizens whose job security was most vulnerable had the most to gain as consumers.&quot; A conjoint or survey experiment on automation attitudes could include a consumer-priming arm to test whether the backlash among displaced workers is attenuated by activating consumer identity.&lt;br/&gt;- Elite messaging experiment (p.1307–1309): Partisan context conditions priming effects. In US automation research, Republican vs. Democratic messaging about trade/robots may similarly gate whether affected workers respond with backlash or acceptance — testable with a vignette crossed with partisan cue.&lt;br/&gt;- Generalizability bounds (p.1309): The paper argues consumer priming works when (a) welfare programs are small and (b) consumer prices are high — conditions that describe the US (small welfare, though food prices moderate). Engage with Hiscox (2006) null result as the US-context contrast case.&lt;br/&gt;- Operationalizing &quot;cheap shopper&quot; in CZ data (p.1305): The Cheap Shopper proxy (no luxury brand ownership) could be approximated in survey data with questions about price sensitivity or store preference — adaptable as a heterogeneity dimension in an automation attitudes survey.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;br/&gt;- The worker/consumer duality is directly portable to automation research: automation-exposed workers are also consumers of cheaper goods enabled by the same technological/trade trends. Ignoring the consumption channel may overstate predicted backlash.&lt;br/&gt;- The priming design offers a blueprint for a conjoint manipulation arm: randomly assign whether automation vignettes emphasize job displacement or consumer price effects, then test HTE by income/job security.&lt;br/&gt;- The paper's mechanism (identity activation rather than information provision) suggests that backlash against automation may be malleable through framing, not just facts — relevant for both positive models of opinion formation and for studying elite discourse effects.&lt;br/&gt;- Partisan mediation finding is important for a voting/backlash project: the effect of automation exposure on anti-trade or anti-globalization voting may be conditional on the partisan messaging environment, not just economic conditions.&lt;br/&gt;- The null result for producer priming (people already think in worker terms by default) has a direct implication: surveys that only ask about job effects of automation will systematically overstate protectionist sentiment relative to a fuller dual-interest measure.&lt;br/&gt;- Hiscox (2006) US contrast case is essential reading alongside this paper — it bounds the conditions under which consumer identity suppresses backlash, and the US context may be closer to the null-consumer-priming case than to Japan.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>automation-project</rdf:value>
