        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2017"/>
        <dc:title>(Mis)Attributing the Causes of American Job Loss</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>What difference does it make if people attribute the loss of manufacturing jobs to trade as opposed to automation? Attributions of responsibility for social problems help shape mass opinion. In this study I use two experiments, including one nationally representative probability survey-experiment, to examine the consequences of attributing job loss to trade versus automation. Findings suggest that as of 2018, public discourse attributes manufacturing job loss in America primarily to trade. When I experimentally manipulate attributions of responsibility for job loss, I find important consequences for levels of mass support for international trade, the extent of negative emotions arising from job loss, and beliefs that trade restrictions and tariffs can bring back manufacturing jobs. Finally, job loss that is attributed to trade—even when it is a single job loss—also serves as a threat to the national ingroup, which triggers a heightened sense of national superiority among white Americans.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021-08-04</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://academic.oup.com/poq/article/85/1/101/6310441</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 14:48:37</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>https://academic.oup.com/journals/pages/open_access/funder_policies/chorus/standard_publication_model</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>101-122</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>mutzMisAttributingCausesAmerican2021</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0033-362X,%201537-5331">
        <prism:volume>85</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>Public Opinion Quarterly</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1093/poq/nfab003</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>1</prism:number>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0033-362X, 1537-5331</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2089">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis — Mutz 2021, (Mis)Attributing the Causes of American Job Loss&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Mutz, Diana C. 2021. &quot;(Mis)Attributing the Causes of American Job Loss: The Consequences of Getting It Wrong.&quot; Public Opinion Quarterly 85(1): 101–122. https://doi.org/10.1093/poq/nfab003&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One-line claim: Attributing manufacturing job loss to trade (vs. automation) makes people angrier, more anti-trade, more convinced tariffs will restore jobs, and more willing to assert American superiority over Chinese people — because trade attribution activates outgroup threat in a way automation does not.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- &quot;misattributed blame for job loss may have important consequences for mass opinion&quot; (p. 101/abstract) — the paper's central problem statement.&lt;br/&gt;- &quot;Both automation and international trade policy are external phenomena that are beyond the individual's control. So why should it matter which is blamed for job loss?&quot; (p. 104) — the puzzle the study answers.&lt;br/&gt;- National experiment headline effects: emotions 11% lower, trade support 6% higher when automation-attributed; assertions of American superiority 6% higher when trade-attributed; belief jobs could return 6% higher (all p &lt; 0.01). (p. 113)&lt;br/&gt;- &quot;Job loss due to trade threatens people's ingroups in a way that job loss due to automation does not.&quot; (p. 118) — the mechanism in one sentence.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Control condition comparison: job loss attributed to trade produced 9% stronger negative emotions vs. no-attribution control; automation condition produced no such effect — isolating the attribution as the active ingredient, not job loss itself. (p. 113–115)&lt;br/&gt;- H5 supported: education moderates the nationalism effect — less-educated respondents (strong national identity) showed a 9% difference in American Superiority by attribution condition. (p. 116)&lt;br/&gt;- Reciprocal causation insight: prior literature treats national superiority as a cause of trade opposition; Mutz shows trade threat also produces nationalism, suggesting a mutually reinforcing cycle. (p. 112)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Acemoglu, Autor, Dorn, Hanson &amp; Price (2016), J Labor Economics — canonical estimates on automation vs. trade as sources of manufacturing job loss. Cited for the &quot;85 percent automation&quot; claim.&lt;br/&gt;- Mutz &amp; Kim (2017), International Organization — ingroup favoritism as the key driver of trade preferences; foundational for this paper's theoretical framework.&lt;br/&gt;- DiTella &amp; Rodrik (2019), Economic Journal — online survey experiments on how labor market shocks increase demand for trade protection. Very close in design to conjoint work.&lt;br/&gt;- Scheve &amp; Slaughter (2001); Mansfield &amp; Mutz (2009) — foundational trade preferences literature; basis for the economic self-interest baseline.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Nexis-Uni corpus (2000–2019, Major US Newspapers, bulk download): documents that trade-linked job loss articles outnumber automation-linked by more than 8:1. Genuinely novel contextual evidence motivating why the experimental manipulation is realistic. (p. 102)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The 3-condition (unattributed / trade / automation) within-vignette design is directly adaptable as a conjoint attribute: &quot;Reason job was eliminated: [trade with China / automation / not stated].&quot; Interact with worker demographics and respondent education to estimate heterogeneous attribution effects.&lt;br/&gt;- The identifiable victim paradigm (NYT worker profile) outperforms statistical framing in generating emotional response; in a conjoint, specificity of profile (named, with biography) may amplify attribute-level effects relative to abstract typologies.&lt;br/&gt;- Attribution cues are a &quot;low counter-argument&quot; persuasion pathway: framing an attribute as automation (rather than trade) raised trade support 6% even in the most anti-trade subgroup. This is a possible mechanism to exploit or test in a conjoint on policy attitudes.&lt;br/&gt;- CZ-level implication: Mutz's mechanism predicts that in high-trade-exposure CZs, the nationalism/xenophobia channel should operate alongside — and perhaps independent of — economic hardship. Heterogeneous effects by CZ racial composition and national identity proxies are testable.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Conjoint attribute design**: &quot;Cause of job displacement&quot; (trade/automation/unspecified) is a validated experimental attribute with known effect sizes (~6–11 pp). Combine with worker industry, wage level, and respondent education.&lt;br/&gt;- **Mechanism for globalization backlash**: The outgroup-threat pathway (trade attribution → ingroup defensiveness → nationalism) is distinct from economic self-interest. Your conjoint can test whether respondents' policy support responds more to framing than to factual exposure levels.&lt;br/&gt;- **Heterogeneous treatment effects**: Less-educated respondents are most reactive; CZ automation exposure may proxy for whether the local discourse frame is trade or technology — a natural moderator in observational CZ-level work.&lt;br/&gt;- **DiTella &amp; Rodrik 2019**: This reference is the closest precursor to a conjoint-style labor shock experiment; read it before finalizing your design.&lt;br/&gt;- **Nationalism as outcome, not just input**: If your survey includes measures of national identity or ethnocentrism, Mutz's reciprocal model predicts these should be elevated in high-trade-exposure areas — testable as additional dependent variables.&lt;br/&gt;- **Media framing as confounder**: The Nexis-Uni result (trade &gt;&gt; automation in coverage) means observational variation in political attitudes may partly reflect media attribution frames, not just actual exposure. Worth noting as a confound in CZ-level identification.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
