        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2021"/>
        <dc:title>Labour Market Shocks and the Demand for Trade Protection: Evidence from Online Surveys</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Abstract
            We study preferences for government action in response to layoffs resulting from different types of labour-market shocks. We consider: technological change, a demand shift, bad management and three kinds of international outsourcing. Support for government intervention rises sharply in response to shocks and is heavily biased towards trade protection. Trade shocks generate more demand for protectionism and, among trade shocks, outsourcing to a developing country elicits greater demand for protectionism. The ‘bad management’ shock is the only scenario that induces a desired increase in compensatory transfers. Trump supporters are more protectionist than Clinton supporters, but preferences seem easy to manipulate: Clinton supporters primed with trade shocks are as protectionist as baseline Trump voters. Highlighting labour abuses in the exporting country increases the demand for trade protection by Clinton supporters but not Trump supporters.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2020-05-01</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Labour Market Shocks and the Demand for Trade Protection</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://academic.oup.com/ej/article/130/628/1008/5714081</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 14:51:15</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>https://academic.oup.com/journals/pages/open_access/funder_policies/chorus/standard_publication_model</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>1008-1030</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>ditellaLabourMarketShocks2020a</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0013-0133,%201468-0297">
        <prism:volume>130</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>The Economic Journal</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1093/ej/ueaa006</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>628</prism:number>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0013-0133, 1468-0297</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2186">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis — Di Tella &amp;amp; Rodrik (2020) Labour Market Shocks and Trade Protection&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;**Di Tella, Rafael and Dani Rodrik. &quot;Labour Market Shocks and the Demand for Trade Protection: Evidence from Online Surveys.&quot; *The Economic Journal* 130, no. 628 (May 2020): 1008–1030. https://doi.org/10.1093/ej/ueaa006**&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;**One-line claim:** When exposed to vignettes about factory closures, Americans demand trade protection far more than compensatory transfers regardless of whether jobs were lost to trade or technology — and the protectionist response is 2–3x larger for trade shocks, especially trade with developing countries, while a simple narrative can transform Clinton voters into Trump-baseline-level protectionists.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. **Protectionist bias across all shocks (p. 1009):** &quot;Support for government intervention rises sharply in response to shocks and is heavily biased towards trade protection. Trade shocks generate more demand for protectionism and, among trade shocks, outsourcing to a developing country elicits greater demand for protectionism.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. **Quantitative magnitude (p. 1009):** &quot;The changes in the desired government response are heavily biased towards trade protection rather than financial assistance. The increase in the demand for government transfers, relative to the control scenario, is small (of 10% or less). Meanwhile the demand for trade protection rises by a magnitude that ranges between 20 and 200%.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. **Causal attribution as the mechanism (p. 1010):** &quot;People do not treat different types of job loss uniformly. They distinguish among labour market shocks according to what produces them; the elasticity of the response depends on the operative mechanism. Non-trade shocks such as technology and demand shocks do increase the demand for protection, but trade shocks elicit a much more protectionist response by a factor between two and three.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. **Narrative priming collapses partisan gap (p. 1011):** &quot;Clinton supporters primed with trade shocks are as protectionist as baseline Trump voters. This finding attests to the power of even relatively simple vignettes to shape preferences over public policy. It is suggestive of the ease with which political campaigns can manipulate policy attitudes by supplying appropriate narrative frames.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting evidence&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Bad management exception (p. 1010):** &quot;An important exception is the case of job losses caused by management failures. In this case, it is the demand for compensatory transfers that goes up without much of a rise in the demand for protection.&quot; — Moral deservingness filters policy instrument choice.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Country identity drives protectionism, not labour standards text (p. 1010):** &quot;Simply changing the name of the country to which production is outsourced, from France to Cambodia, increases the demand for import protection by six percentage points (which is more than half the baseline level of demand for trade protection).&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Trump 3x more elastic to trade shocks (p. 1026):** &quot;When primed with unemployment caused by a trade shock, Trump supporters increase their preference for protectionism by 33 percentage points on average, while Clinton supporters increase their preference for protectionism only by nine percentage points on average.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Education moderates only at post-graduate level (p. 1028):** &quot;The trade shocks push up the demand for trade protection by roughly 20 percentage points for less- and medium-educated groups. For the most educated groups, the increase in the demand for trade protection is only eight percentage points, and the change is not statistically different from zero.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Autor, Dorn, Hanson &amp; Majlesi (2016)** — &quot;Importing Political Polarization?&quot; — electoral consequences of rising trade exposure; companion to this paper's demand-side work.&lt;br/&gt;- **Colantone &amp; Stanig (2017)** — trade origins of economic nationalism, voting behaviour in Western Europe.&lt;br/&gt;- **Guiso, Herrera, Morelli &amp; Sonno (2017)** — demand and supply of populism; CEPR DP11871.&lt;br/&gt;- **Acemoglu et al. (2016)** — China trade shock = only 10% of manufacturing job loss in 2000s; establishes the &quot;automation &gt; trade&quot; empirical baseline challenged by this paper's political findings.&lt;br/&gt;- **Scheve &amp; Slaughter (2001); Mayda &amp; Rodrik (2005)** — foundational work on individual trade-policy preferences; important baseline for conjoint design.&lt;br/&gt;- **Kuziemko et al. (2015)** — mTurk experiments on redistribution preferences; direct methodological comparator for online vignette/conjoint designs.&lt;br/&gt;- **Grossman &amp; Helpman (1994)** — &quot;Protection for Sale&quot; — material-interest model that this paper empirically challenges.&lt;br/&gt;- **Rodrik (2014)** — &quot;When Ideas Trump Interests&quot; — theoretical backdrop; ideas/narratives reshape policy preferences.&lt;br/&gt;- **DellaVigna &amp; Gentzkow (2010)** — persuasion rates framework; persuasion rate formula used to benchmark treatment effects.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Novel 6-treatment vignette design (p. 1011):** Randomized newspaper-article vignettes holding labour-market outcome constant (plant closure, 900 jobs) while varying the causal mechanism across 6 arms (tech shock, demand shock, bad management, outsourcing to France, outsourcing to Cambodia, Cambodia + poor labour standards), then eliciting a three-way policy choice (nothing / transfers / protection). Genuinely novel in combining multiple non-trade and trade shock types with competing policy options in a single design. N = 5,685 US adults via mTurk, two waves June–July 2018.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. **Hold-outcome-constant vignette design (p. 1009):** &quot;we study how opinions about trade protectionism and compensatory financial transfers change when individuals are presented with six different types of shocks, all of which have the same effect on local labour markets.&quot; — A conjoint experiment could adopt this logic: fix job-loss magnitude, vary cause (automation / offshoring / demand decline) and worker/firm characteristics as conjoint attributes to measure preference weights.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. **Single-word swap for automation (p. 1018):** &quot;T4 and T5 are identical except for a single word, which is the name of the country.&quot; — Replicate with &quot;a robot&quot; vs. &quot;a worker in Cambodia&quot; to isolate pure attribution effect. Would test whether automation triggers same protectionist bias or a different policy response (retraining vs. tariffs).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. **Procedural fairness as the construct to operationalize (p. 1030):** &quot;It would be especially useful to unpack further the procedural fairness frames through which people evaluate different labour market shocks and policy options.&quot; — Conjoint attributes could vary cause, victim deservingness, and firm culpability to decompose fairness from material interest. Maps directly onto your research question about blame attribution in automation vs. trade job loss.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. **Priming/framing effects benchmark (p. 1012):** &quot;worldviews can be shaped by priming — exposure to news stories with a particular slant.&quot; The 20% persuasion rate for trade-shock protectionism (high end of marketing literature) means framing effects are large enough to be detectable in conjoint samples; power calculations should account for this range.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;5. **CZ-level heterogeneity extension:** Paper does not exploit geographic variation in trade/automation exposure. A natural extension: does local CZ-level automation or import penetration (Autor/Acemoglu measures) predict baseline protectionism or differential response elasticity to shock-type vignettes? Could be tested by merging respondent ZIP codes with CZ-level exposure measures.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;6. **Partisan heterogeneity design implication:** Trump supporters are 3x more elastic to trade shock priming. In a conjoint, party ID should be a key moderator in the analysis plan, not just a covariate. A pre-registered interaction between party ID and shock-cause attribute could be the headline HTE.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Attribution matters more than outcomes:** This paper's core result — identical job losses generate radically different policy demands depending on perceived cause — is the micro-level mechanism underlying CZ-level evidence that trade shocks (but not automation) produce political backlash. Your conjoint can test this attribution logic directly at the individual level.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Automation gets less blame than trade:** Even a technology shock raises protectionist demand only 5–6pp vs. 14–20pp for trade shocks. This suggests automation may be politically &quot;safer&quot; for incumbents — worth testing whether this asymmetry extends to electoral accountability or policy support in a conjoint.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Developing-country trade is uniquely toxic:** The Cambodia vs. France swap (+6pp, p &lt; 0.01) shows that country identity, not just &quot;foreignness,&quot; drives protectionist demand. A conjoint could vary the trade partner (rich country / middle-income / low-wage country) as an attribute to estimate this premium precisely.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Narrative priming is powerful and partisan:** The result that Clinton voters become as protectionist as baseline Trump voters after a trade narrative has direct implications for conjoint framing: the order and labelling of attributes may matter more than in typical preference experiments. Randomize vignette order and pre-register robustness checks.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Policy instrument menu matters:** The finding that demand shifts to protection (not transfers) even for technology shocks suggests that if conjoint attributes include policy responses, framing &quot;retraining&quot; vs. &quot;tariffs&quot; as options may reveal whether automation uniquely activates a retraining preference that trade shocks do not.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **mTurk validation:** The paper validates mTurk as a platform for trade-attitude experiments and shows it is broadly representative on key covariates (though younger and more educated). Useful precedent for your own conjoint sampling and any reviewer concerns about external validity.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
