    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2135">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis — Magistro et al. (2024)&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Magistro, Beatrice, Peter Loewen, Bart Bonikowski, Sophie Borwein, and Blake Lee-Whiting. &quot;Attitudes toward Automation and the Demand for Policies Addressing Job Loss: The Effects of Information about Trade-offs.&quot; Political Science Research and Methods 12, no. 4 (2024): 783–798. https://doi.org/10.1017/psrm.2024.1&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One-line claim: Information about automation's trade-offs — especially consumer price reductions — significantly raises perceptions of fairness toward firms that automate, but leaves policy preferences unchanged, suggesting that automation's political salience depends on how it is framed rather than on material exposure alone.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Core claim (p. 785): Despite relatively fixed policy preferences, fairness evaluations of automation are sensitive to trade-off information, especially price changes — the mechanism linking framing to political salience.&lt;br/&gt;- Headline conjoint result (p. 794): Price decreases of 20–50% drive the largest shifts in perceived fairness (mean 3.52 vs. 3.19 on a 5-point scale at 50% vs. 0% price drop); prices dominate all other conjoint attributes.&lt;br/&gt;- Key null result (p. 793): No treatment — costs-only, generic trade-off, or specific conjoint — moves any of the eight measured policy preferences; preferences are sticky regardless of information condition.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Unexpected finding (p. 792): Vague trade-off framing (&quot;some jobs gained, some lost, prices could fall&quot;) raises fairness perceptions more than specific numeric trade-offs, because respondents optimistically fill in blanks when information is imprecise.&lt;br/&gt;- Loss asymmetry (p. 795): Respondents react to wage declines but not wage increases — consistent with prospect theory; losses loom larger than equivalent gains in automation attitudes.&lt;br/&gt;- Cross-study replication (p. 785): Retraining workers is the most popular policy response across all treatment groups; restricting skilled immigration and trade are the least popular — replicates Di Tella &amp; Rodrik (2020) and Gallego et al. (2023).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Anelli, Colantone &amp; Stanig (2019), IZA DP 12485: &quot;We were the robots&quot; — automation and voting in Western Europe. Core empirical baseline for automation-voting link.&lt;br/&gt;- Borwein, Bonikowski, Loewen et al. (2024a), West European Politics: Perceived automation threat, populism, and vote choice across 15 European democracies. Direction of vote depends on how technological threat is understood.&lt;br/&gt;- Rodrik (2018), JIBP: Populism and the economics of globalization — unfairness (not inequality) drives anti-globalization sentiment. Theoretical bridge between trade and automation backlash.&lt;br/&gt;- Di Tella &amp; Rodrik (2020), EJ: Labor market shock primes and demand for trade protection; trade effects stronger than automation effects on protectionism. Foundational comparison design for conjoint work.&lt;br/&gt;- Mutz (2021), POQ: Misattributed blame for job loss — automation often blamed on trade/immigration. Key for understanding displaced blame in automation/trade research.&lt;br/&gt;- Wu (2021), PSRM: &quot;Misattributed blame? Attitudes toward globalization in the age of automation.&quot; Directly tests automation-vs.-trade misattribution.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Novel conjoint attribute (p. 786): First experimental study to include consumer price changes as an explicit cost/benefit of automation in a conjoint design — and prices turn out to be the most influential attribute. Standard labor-market attributes (employment, wages) are secondary.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Framing treatment design: The costs-only vs. generic trade-off vs. specific conjoint structure is directly replicable for trade shocks. A conjoint on import competition could vary price effects for consumers alongside job displacement in affected industries — this paper shows price information will likely dominate.&lt;br/&gt;- Consumer price channel for CZ research: CZ-level automation exposure measures (Acemoglu &amp; Restrepo robots; Dorn-Hanson trade exposure) focus on labor-market outcomes. Adding a local consumer-price exposure measure (e.g., import price indices by product category, matched to CZ consumption baskets) could test whether the price channel modulates political responses to automation/trade.&lt;br/&gt;- Policy stickiness as a design consideration: If information treatments do not move policy preferences (only fairness perceptions), own conjoint dependent variables should include both fairness/legitimacy items and policy support items — and the former may be more sensitive.&lt;br/&gt;- Elite framing and salience: Automation is currently a low-salience issue. Own research could exploit variation in automation's media salience across CZs or over time as a moderator of political effects — the paper explicitly suggests persistent political messaging would amplify effects.&lt;br/&gt;- Prospect theory in conjoint attribute design: Build in asymmetry — test whether job loss attributes weigh more heavily than equal-sized job gain attributes in conjoint ratings of trade/automation policies.&lt;br/&gt;- Multi-country design template: Cint quota sample (~2000/country) across 4 liberal market economies provides a clean methodological template for a cross-national conjoint on automation and trade attitudes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The paper directly addresses conjoint experiment design for automation attitudes: the specific conjoint table structure (firm type × price change × high-skill employment × low-skill employment × wages) is adaptable for a trade-automation comparison conjoint.&lt;br/&gt;- The price-dominance finding is the most important result for CZ-level research: standard automation-exposure measures ignore the consumer side; adding a local price-impact measure to CZ regressions could explain why automation's political effects are weaker and more heterogeneous than trade's.&lt;br/&gt;- Policy stickiness (null treatment effects on policy preferences) is a caution for own conjoint DV selection — fairness/legitimacy perceptions may be the better outcome variable if testing framing effects rather than information effects on policy demand.&lt;br/&gt;- The misattribution literature (Mutz, Wu) cited here is essential context: workers may attribute automation-driven job loss to trade or immigration, which means automation-exposure effects in observational CZ regressions could be partly capturing trade/immigration blame, not just automation threat per se.&lt;br/&gt;- The vague-vs.-specific framing paradox has implications for how survey vignettes in own conjoint describe trade shocks: keeping descriptions qualitatively vague may produce larger attitude effects than providing precise import penetration statistics.&lt;br/&gt;- Borwein et al. (2024a) — a companion paper by the same team on automation threat and populist voting across 15 European democracies — is the closest existing study to a CZ-style analysis of automation and voting and should be read alongside this paper.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
