        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2029"/>
        <dc:title>The Backlash Against Globalization</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>In recent years, the world has seen a rising backlash against globalization. This article reviews the nature, causes, and consequences of the globalization backlash. It shows that, contrary to a popular narrative, the backlash is not associated with a large swing in public opinion against globalization but is rather a result of its politicization. The increasing influence of globalization-skeptic actors has resulted in more protectionist, isolationist, and nationalist policies, some of which fundamentally threaten pillars of the contemporary international order. Both material and nonmaterial causes drive the globalization backlash, and these causes interact and mediate each other. The consequences are shaped by the responses of societal actors, national governments, and international policy makers. These responses can either yield to and reinforce the global backlash or push back against it. Understanding these dynamics will be an important task for future research.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021-05-11</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.annualreviews.org/doi/10.1146/annurev-polisci-041719-102405</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 14:56:35</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:rights>http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/</dc:rights>
        <bib:pages>421-442</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>walterBacklashGlobalization2021</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2189">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis — Walter 2021, The Backlash Against Globalization&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Walter, Stefanie. &quot;The Backlash Against Globalization.&quot; Annual Review of Political Science 24 (2021): 421–442. https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-polisci-041719-102405&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One-line claim: The globalization backlash is not a mass preference shift but a politicization of latent antiglobalization attitudes by elites and parties, producing electoral and policy consequences disproportionate to any actual change in public opinion.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. (p. 421/abstract) &quot;the backlash is not associated with a large swing in public opinion against globalization but is rather a result of its politicization. The increasing influence of globalization-skeptic actors has resulted in more protectionist, isolationist, and nationalist policies, some of which fundamentally threaten pillars of the contemporary international order.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. (p. 425) &quot;Although voters' attitudes about globalization have not changed much, they increasingly vote for candidates, parties, or proposals that oppose globalization. What explains this apparent paradox? An important part of the answer is the increasing politicization of issues related to globalization.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. (p. 434/conclusion) &quot;the globalization backlash is not associated with a large swing in public opinion against globalization. Rather, existing antiglobalization attitudes have been politicized and become more politically consequential.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. (p. 425) &quot;voters' support for political parties promoting liberal internationalist policies in the West has almost halved since the 1990s (Trubowitz &amp; Burgoon 2020). Moreover, the share of referendums decided against international cooperation has increased substantially since the 2000s, with every second referendum ending in favor of noncooperation in the 2010s (De Vries et al. 2021).&quot; — Behavioral shift without opinion shift is the core empirical puzzle.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. (p. 431) &quot;several studies find that support for radical right parties and nationalist projects such as Brexit is driven by exposure to automation and the digital revolution (Colantone &amp; Stanig 2019, Frey et al. 2018, Im et al. 2019). In fact, the IMF (2019) concludes in a recent study that the effects of technology on local labor markets are much more pervasive and long-lasting than trade shocks.&quot; — Automation may outweigh trade in generating backlash.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. Colantone &amp; Stanig 2018a — China shock → radical right vote shares in Europe (CZ-level)&lt;br/&gt;2. Dippel et al. 2015 — trade competition and far-right in Germany (CZ-level)&lt;br/&gt;3. Dorn et al. 2020 — import competition and ideological polarization (US CZ-level)&lt;br/&gt;4. Feigenbaum &amp; Hall 2015 — trade and legislator protectionism (US CZ-level)&lt;br/&gt;5. Frey et al. 2018 — automation exposure and voting behavior&lt;br/&gt;6. Im et al. 2019 — digital revolution and backlash&lt;br/&gt;7. Rommel &amp; Walter 2018 — individual offshoring risk and populist voting (losers-don't-vote puzzle)&lt;br/&gt;8. Walter et al. 2018 — biased expectations in globalization referendums&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[None. This is a review article with no original dataset or novel identification strategy. Walter assembles ISSP survey data and Comparative Manifesto Project data for descriptive figures, but these are workhorse secondary sources, not novel contributions.]&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. CONJOINT FRAMING: Walter argues individuals are &quot;overly optimistic about the consequences&quot; of deglobalization (p. 430). A conjoint experiment could randomize the described consequences of trade restriction (jobs returned vs. consumer prices raised vs. retaliation) to test which frame drives support — directly operationalizing the &quot;expectations&quot; mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. ELITE CUE RANDOMIZATION: &quot;political elites can strategically mobilize voters with globalization-skeptic attitudes by using antiglobalization messages&quot; (p. 433). A conjoint could vary whether the trade/automation policy is attributed to a left vs. right politician and use job-loss framing vs. sovereignty framing — testing which cue activates latent attitudes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. MEASUREMENT PROBLEM AS CONJOINT MOTIVATION: Walter notes that &quot;identifying individuals' objective, respondent-specific material interest is much more difficult&quot; in observational data (p. 432). Conjoint experiments solve exactly this — randomized exposure scenarios bypass endogenous selection into affected industries/regions.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. OUTCOME SPLITTING: The welfare-state finding (p. 436) — strong-welfare-state countries experience backlash on sociocultural but not economic globalization — suggests splitting the dependent variable in CZ-level automation work between economic attitudes (trade, redistribution) and sociocultural attitudes (immigration, national identity).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;5. POLITICIZATION AS MODERATOR: Since backlash is driven by politicization not preferences, local political context (presence of a strong anti-globalization party) could be a moderator in CZ-level automation regressions — testing whether the same trade/automation shock produces larger effects in politically contested districts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- The central finding (politicization, not preference change) implies that the causal chain runs: structural shock → elite mobilization → voting change, not shock → attitude change → voting change. Any conjoint or CZ-level design should account for local political supply-side conditions, not just exposure.&lt;br/&gt;- Walter explicitly flags automation as potentially more consequential than trade for local labor markets (citing Frey et al. 2018, IMF 2019). This directly legitimizes an automation-centered CZ analysis as a complement to China-shock work.&lt;br/&gt;- The &quot;losers don't vote accordingly&quot; puzzle (Rommel &amp; Walter 2018; Colantone &amp; Stanig 2018a,b) means individual-level exposure measures will underperform in voting regressions — CZ-level aggregation or conjoint randomization are the better identification strategies.&lt;br/&gt;- Counterfactual expectations (Walter et al. 2018) are a mechanism conjoint experiments can directly test by varying the described outcome of automation-driven job loss (retraining available vs. permanent displacement vs. community decline).&lt;br/&gt;- The review distinguishes economic, political, and sociocultural globalization dimensions — worth keeping as separate outcomes in conjoint analysis rather than collapsing into a single &quot;globalization support&quot; scale.&lt;br/&gt;- The failure of compensation strategies even in generous welfare states (Denmark, Sweden) is a useful null result for the policy implications section of papers arguing automation → redistribution demand.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>
           <z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>automation</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag>
