        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2032"/>
        <dc:title>Validating vignette and conjoint survey experiments against real-world behavior</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Survey experiments, like vignette and conjoint analyses, are widely used in the social sciences to elicit stated preferences and study how humans make multidimensional choices. However, there is a paucity of research on the external validity of these methods that examines whether the determinants that explain hypothetical choices made by survey respondents match the determinants that explain what subjects actually do when making similar choices in real-world situations. This study compares results from conjoint and vignette analyses on which immigrant attributes generate support for naturalization with closely corresponding behavioral data from a natural experiment in Switzerland, where some municipalities used referendums to decide on the citizenship applications of foreign residents. Using a representative sample from the same population and the official descriptions of applicant characteristics that voters received before each referendum as a behavioral benchmark, we find that the effects of the applicant attributes estimated from the survey experiments perform remarkably well in recovering the effects of the same attributes in the behavioral benchmark. We also find important differences in the relative performances of the different designs. Overall, the paired conjoint design, where respondents evaluate two immigrants side by side, comes closest to the behavioral benchmark; on average, its estimates are within 2% percentage points of the effects in the behavioral benchmark.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2015-02-24</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>pnas.org (Atypon)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1416587112</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 14:57:07</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>2395-2400</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>hainmuellerValidatingVignetteConjoint2015</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2088">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis — Hainmueller, Hangartner &amp;amp; Yamamoto (2015)&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hainmueller, Jens, Dominik Hangartner, and Teppei Yamamoto. 2015. &quot;Validating Vignette and Conjoint Survey Experiments against Real-World Behavior.&quot; Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 112 (8): 2395–2400.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One-line claim: Conjoint and vignette survey experiments recover real-world behavioral attribute effects remarkably well — the paired conjoint design comes closest, with estimates within 2 percentage points of a behavioral benchmark — primarily because paired presentation reduces satisficing and increases respondent engagement.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Red — Key points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. (p. 2395) Headline finding: the paired conjoint design, where respondents evaluate two immigrants side by side, comes closest to the behavioral benchmark; on average, its estimates are within 2 percentage points of the effects in the behavioral benchmark.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. (p. 2399) Core mechanism: attribute effects decrease almost monotonically as designs become worse-performing, and in proportion to satisficing rates — nondifferentially accepting all applicants mechanically shrinks any attribute effect toward zero.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. (p. 2400) Prescriptive conclusion: to maximize external validity, survey samples must match the target population and designs must motivate genuine engagement with the hypothetical choice task.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Yellow — Important supporting evidence&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- (p. 2399) Satisficing rates by design: 56% (paired conjoint) → 63% (paired vignette) → 70–72% (single-profile designs) — directly explains the performance gradient.&lt;br/&gt;- (p. 2400) The single-vignette design — the most widely used in social science — is the weakest performer among main designs, despite most closely resembling the real-world format.&lt;br/&gt;- (p. 2398) Conjoint recovers relative attribute effects well but systematically underpredicts absolute rejection levels (~21% predicted vs. 37% observed) — marginal effects are reliable; absolute levels are not.&lt;br/&gt;- (p. 2400) Convenience (student) sample with identical design fared poorly — sample composition matters as much as design.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Orange — References to follow up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Hainmueller, Hopkins &amp; Yamamoto (2014) &quot;Causal inference in conjoint analysis&quot; Polit Anal 22(1):1–30 — foundational AMCE methodology paper; must-read for own conjoint design.&lt;br/&gt;- Hainmueller &amp; Hangartner (2013) &quot;Who gets a Swiss passport?&quot; APSR 107(1):159–187 — the behavioral benchmark study; natural experiment on immigrant attribute effects.&lt;br/&gt;- Bertrand &amp; Mullainathan (2001) &quot;Do people mean what they say?&quot; AER 91(2):67–72 — canonical paper on gap between stated and revealed preferences; directly relevant to conjoint on globalization attitudes.&lt;br/&gt;- Krosnick, Judd &amp; Wittenbrink (2014) Handbook of Attitudes — satisficing and acquiescence response bias; foundational for understanding why design affects data quality.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- First external validation of vignette and conjoint designs against a behavioral benchmark in social science. The natural experiment (Swiss naturalization referendums with secret ballots, exogenous variation in applicant attributes, known information environment) is an unusually clean real-world benchmark — most prior work only compared aggregate market shares, not attribute-level causal effects.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Green — Thought starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. Use paired conjoint (not single-profile) for own automation/trade attitudes experiment. The 60% longer deliberation time with no increase in reported burden means better data quality at no cost in respondent experience.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. Don't optimize for face similarity to real-world policy formats (e.g., mimicking a layoff notice or TAA application). Engagement eclipses superficial resemblance — prioritize side-by-side comparison that forces differentiation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. Generalizability warrant: this validation used a hot-button, socially sensitive issue (immigration, origin-based discrimination) with a 10-year gap between survey and behavioral data and still held up. This supports confidence in using conjoint for automation/globalization backlash — also sensitive, also politically charged.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. Sample from trade-exposed commuting zones (CZs) using probability methods, not MTurk/opt-in panels. The student-sample failure is a direct warning: a representative sample of the target population (e.g., workers in high-automation-exposure CZs) is essential for the estimates to correspond to real-world behavior.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;5. The absolute-level caveat is important: conjoint will reliably tell you which attributes of a trade/automation shock (sector, cause, duration, policy response) matter more or less, but will not accurately predict the unconditional fraction of workers who support redistribution or retraining — interpret AMCEs, not predicted probabilities as population shares.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;6. When comparing conjoint designs for own pilot, consider running both paired conjoint and paired vignette as a within-pilot comparison — they tie on the Bonferroni-corrected significance metric while differing in format, which can inform the final instrument.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;br/&gt;## Relevance to automation/trade/voting project — bottom line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Conjoint design validation**: This paper is the key methodological warrant for using a paired conjoint to study trade/automation job-loss attitudes. Cite it whenever defending conjoint external validity.&lt;br/&gt;- **Design choice**: Use paired conjoint (not single-profile vignette) to minimize satisficing. This is the clearest design recommendation in the literature.&lt;br/&gt;- **Social desirability robustness**: Even on a sensitive issue (immigration origin discrimination), conjoint recovered structural effects — provides confidence for politically charged automation/globalization attitudes.&lt;br/&gt;- **Sample design**: Probability sample of the relevant target population (trade-exposed workers, not MTurk) is essential; this paper provides the empirical basis for that requirement.&lt;br/&gt;- **Interpretation discipline**: AMCEs (attribute-level effects) are reliable; absolute acceptance/rejection levels are not — be careful when converting AMCE estimates to policy-relevant magnitudes.&lt;br/&gt;- **Benchmark idea**: The Swiss referendum natural experiment suggests a research design opportunity — if any real-world policy vote or revealed-preference data exist on trade/automation (e.g., union votes, TAA take-up by automation exposure), they could serve as a behavioral benchmark for a similar validation exercise.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[generation date: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>
           <z:AutomaticTag><rdf:value>conjoint</rdf:value></z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>external-validity</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
