        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_2152"/>
        <dc:title>Robots, Foreigners, and Foreign Robots: Policy Responses to Automation and Trade</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2025-07-29</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>Robots, Foreigners, and Foreign Robots</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>DOI.org (Crossref)</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1086/737849</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-04-29 17:50:35</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>737849</bib:pages>
        <z:citationKey>chaudoinRobotsForeignersForeign2025</z:citationKey>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0022-3816,%201468-2508">
        <dc:title>The Journal of Politics</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1086/737849</dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:alternative>The Journal of Politics</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0022-3816, 1468-2508</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_2207">
        <rdf:value>&lt;h1&gt;Synthesis: Chaudoin &amp;amp; Mangini (2025) — Robots, Foreigners, and Foreign Robots&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;**Chaudoin, Stephen, and Michael-David Mangini. 2025. &quot;Robots, Foreigners, and Foreign Robots: Policy Responses to Automation and Trade.&quot; *Journal of Politics*. https://doi.org/10.1086/737849.**&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;**One-line claim:** The perceived domestic vs. foreign origin of an economic shock — not the shock's distributional consequences — determines whether citizens prefer backpedaling policies (tariffs, automation regulations) or redistributive transfers, because economic nationalism makes regulation of foreign-origin shocks intrinsically attractive and regulation of domestic-origin shocks intrinsically costly.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Red — Key Points&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Abstract/p.1:** &quot;Most importantly, 'making automation foreign' by emphasizing foreign-origin technology re-weights responses towards greater support for regulations and away from redistribution.&quot; — The paper's most novel result: foreignness framing of automation triggers the same regulatory demand as globalization.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **p.3:** &quot;Put simply, the perceived origin of economic dislocation – domestic versus foreign – affects how much weight citizens place on backpedaling policies versus redistribution in their preferred response.&quot; — The central mechanism; the entire formal model and experimental design pivot on this.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **p.5:** &quot;Our research suggests that citizens will demand regulations as the perceived 'foreignness' of technology increases.&quot; — Forward-looking implication: China's rise in robotics and AI could shift US automation politics from redistribution toward direct regulation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **p.30 (Discussion):** &quot;Our paper sheds light on why globalization, not automation, triggered political reactions, and why that reaction de-emphasized redistribution. Economic nationalism, which values exports over imports, makes citizens prefer tariffs for globalization and redistribution for automation.&quot; — Headline answer to the book-end puzzle opened in the introduction.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Yellow — Important Supporting Evidence&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **p.7 (Lit Review):** &quot;Di Tella and Rodrik (2020) and Naoi (2020) survey US and Japanese respondents, respectively. They find that prompts about globalization shocks raise support for protectionism, but *decrease* support for compensation.&quot; — Prior finding the model explains: nationalism substitutes backpedaling for redistribution.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **p.24 (Exp. 1 Results):** &quot;Consistent with the first prediction, foreign shocks caused respondents to place greater relative weight on backpedaling, substituting away from transfers. Going from foreign labor to domestic automation shocks increases the weight on transfers.&quot; — Experiment 1 confirms Prediction 1 across the 2×2 design.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **p.28 (Exp. 2 Results):** &quot;The foreign reliance and relative gains treatments have the strongest effects. The within-country treatment also increases the weight respondents place on regulation, but we cannot reject the null of no effect for that particular treatment in some specifications.&quot; — Security and relative-gains aspects of nationalism drive regulation demand; racialized within-country concerns are the weakest channel.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Orange — References to Follow Up&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Wu (2022)** &quot;Misattributed Blame? Attitudes Toward Globalization in the Age of Automation.&quot; *Political Science Research Methods* 10(3): 470–87. — Core prior on blame misattribution: automation-exposed workers oppose globalization more. C&amp;M's paper explains *why* this misattribution is politically effective.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Wu (2023)** &quot;'Restrict Foreigners, Not Robots': Partisan Responses to Automation Threat.&quot; *Economics &amp; Politics* 35(2): 505–28. — Finds automation prompts raise tariff support even more than China-offshoring prompts. Directly addressed by C&amp;M's foreignness mechanism.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Kuo et al. (2024)** &quot;Automation Versus Openness: Support for Policies to Address Job Threats.&quot; *Journal of Public Policy* 44(1): 1–23. — Finds automation risk does not raise ex post redistribution support — the puzzle C&amp;M explain.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Gallego &amp; Kurer (2022)** &quot;Automation, Digitalization, and Artificial Intelligence in the Workplace: Implications for Political Behavior.&quot; *Annual Review of Political Science* 25: 463–484. — Survey of the politics-of-automation literature; useful entry point and cites non-redistribution finding.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Di Tella &amp; Rodrik (2020)** &quot;Labour Market Shocks and the Demand for Trade Protection.&quot; *Economic Journal* 130(628): 1008–30. — Key experiment: globalization primes crowd out redistribution. Methodological template.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Baccini, Ciobanu &amp; Pelc (2023)** &quot;Why Different Economic Shocks Have Different Political Effects.&quot; — Compares racial perceptions of globalization vs. automation harms; directly relevant for heterogeneous treatment effects in a conjoint design.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Carnegie &amp; Gaikwad (2022)** &quot;Public Opinion on Geopolitics and Trade.&quot; *World Politics.* — Documents adversary-aversion in trade preferences; relevant to geopolitical framing as a conjoint attribute.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Purple — Data &amp; Methods&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **p.9 (Sec. 2.1):** Original data on US automation technology trade deficit using COMTRADE and Harmonized System tariff classification rulings (Mangini 2023): the deficit grew 1472% from ~$180M (1990) to $2.8B (2020), outpacing the overall trade deficit (1015% growth). This is novel empirical context establishing that &quot;foreign robots&quot; is a real and growing phenomenon, not just a hypothetical. Source: Mangini (2023) *Economics &amp; Politics* 35(3): 773–805.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Green — Thought Starters&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. **2×2 shock-type × shock-origin vignette design** (p.20): The news-article vignette crossing automation/labor × domestic/foreign is a clean template replicable in a conjoint. Adding shock origin (domestic US firm vs. Chinese firm vs. German firm) as a conjoint attribute could directly replicate and extend C&amp;M's key result in a multi-attribute setting, while allowing heterogeneous effects by economic nationalism score.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. **Policy bundles as substitutes — relative DV construction** (p.19): C&amp;M measure *differences* (backpedaling minus transfers), not levels. For a conjoint or CZ-level study, this implies the dependent variable of interest is the *ratio* of support for direct regulation vs. redistribution, not their independent levels. This is an important design constraint.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. **Embedded liberalism and economic nationalism interaction** (p.6): C&amp;M show that nationalism crowds out redistribution demand even when redistribution would financially help the nationalist. In a CZ-level design, interacting local automation exposure with a county-level economic nationalism index (e.g., Trump vote share, Mutz-style out-group anxiety) could test whether high-nationalism CZs show weaker redistribution demand despite equal automation exposure.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. **Foreignness as a treatment dimension for AI/white-collar automation** (p.30): C&amp;M note the same logic applies beyond manufacturing — &quot;a politician courting pharmacists displaced by automation... could emphasize the foreignness of imported machinery.&quot; A conjoint could vary whether the AI tool displacing a worker is described as domestically vs. foreign-developed, testing if the foreignness mechanism generalizes to high-skill/white-collar workers.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;5. **Security-reliance vs. relative-gains channels** (p.28): The two strongest sub-treatments in Exp. 2 are *foreign reliance* (dependency/sovereignty risk) and *relative gains* (trading partner benefits more). These map onto distinct conjoint attributes — framing job loss as &quot;making us dependent on China&quot; vs. &quot;China gains more than we lose&quot; — and could be tested as separate attribute levels.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;6. **Pre-treatment economic nationalism moderator** (p.28): C&amp;M show that the foreignness treatment effect is near zero for respondents who view imports favorably (pre-treatment &quot;imports good&quot; &gt; 60 on 0–100 scale). This suggests economic nationalism/import attitudes should be a pre-treatment covariate (or a blocking variable) in any conjoint design building on this paper.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;---&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;## Relevance to Automation/Trade/Voting Project — Bottom Line&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Core puzzle this resolves:** Why does automation exposure not generate the same political backlash as trade exposure? C&amp;M's answer — economic nationalism makes backpedaling against domestic-origin shocks unattractive — directly informs your project's framing of why CZ-level automation exposure may not translate into conventional protectionist or anti-incumbent voting.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Conjoint design implication:** Add &quot;origin of the technology&quot; (domestic US firm / European firm / Chinese firm) as an attribute in any conjoint on automation job loss. The paper predicts this single attribute will shift responses from redistribution to regulation, which is a testable, novel finding for your design.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Dependent variable construction:** Use *relative* support (regulation minus redistribution) rather than independent measures of each policy. C&amp;M demonstrate that absolute levels are noisy and context-dependent; the ratio is theoretically motivated and more robust.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Heterogeneous effects:** Interact automation exposure with economic nationalism scores. C&amp;M predict the foreignness effect is driven by reliance and relative-gains concerns, not racial identity — so measures of import aversion or security concern (rather than just partisan ID) are the right moderators.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **Embedded liberalism connection:** The model predicts that as US automation becomes more associated with foreign firms (China's tech rise), the embedded liberalism bargain — accept dislocation in exchange for a safety net — will erode further. This is a macro prediction testable with cross-national panel data on welfare preferences and automation import exposure.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- **EU comparison:** C&amp;M predict EU members (technology importers) regulate automation more readily because doing so does not harm domestic competitiveness. A cross-national conjoint comparing US and EU respondents on the same foreignness treatment would be a natural extension.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;[Generated: 2026-04-29]&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:value>
